# WHO COLLECTS THIS DIRECTORY, said out loud rather than left to the shape of a filename.
#
# engine/features.mjs runs everything under engine/tests that run-test262 collects — recursive, `.js`, not
# `_FIXTURE.js` — and asserts that the number it RAN equals the number on disk, so a fixture dropped in this
# tree cannot be green without having run. Everything that rule does not reach must say who reaches it, or it
# is an excluded test and features.mjs fails naming the file. §Testing: "A TEST FILE THE GATE DOES NOT COLLECT
# IS AN EXCLUDED TEST … worse, because the total LOOKS complete."
#
# These are the solver differential's documents, not ECMAScript fixtures. Each is a page whose frontier drains,
# run under several schedules by engine/solvergate.mjs, WHICH TAKES ITS CORPUS FROM THE CLAIM BELOW: it calls
# engine/gate_collect.mjs's `claimedCorpus` with its own path, so the suffix on that line is not a DESCRIPTION
# of what the runner enumerates — it IS what the runner enumerates. Edit it and the run changes with it.
#
# THIS USED TO READ "it enumerates them with `readdirSync(CORPUS).filter(f => f.endsWith(\".html\"))`, which is
# the claim below", and it is rewritten rather than deleted because that sentence was ACCURATE and was the
# problem. The suffix was spelled twice — here and in the runner — and the accounting that reads this file
# checks exactly one thing about the runner a claim names: that it EXISTS. Nothing compared the two spellings,
# so narrowing either would have left these documents claimed by a line and run by nobody, with the calling
# gate green over a corpus it never asked about. A reader who re-derives "say what the runner does" writes
# that sentence again; there is one copy now and the runner reads it.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT CHECKED ANYWHERE: that engine/solvergate.mjs is ever INVOKED. It is a hand-run bulk gate
# and no stage of engine/build.mjs runs it. A claim is honoured by its runner; whether the runner runs is a
# fact about the build's stage list, and this file cannot see it.
#
# `claimedCorpus` READS ONE DIRECTORY, AND THIS CLAIM IS THEREFORE ABOUT THIS DIRECTORY ONLY. (It said
# "THAT ENUMERATION" while the sentence it pointed at named a `readdirSync` call; a reference by POSITION
# survives the rewrite of what it points at and goes on reading as authoritative, so it carries the
# name now.) A GATE does
# not speak for a subdirectory, precisely because the runner it names need not descend and this one does not:
# a document at solver/<anything>/x.html would be claimed by a claim that inherits and enumerated by nobody —
# an excluded test whose exclusion is stated nowhere, reached THROUGH the accounting that exists to end it. So
# a subdirectory here writes its own GATE, or its content is .js and run-test262 collects it recursively.
#
# Format: <runner path from the repo root>  <suffix or exact filename>

engine/solvergate.mjs  .html
